## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable data lifecycle with multi-tenant governance and cost control; integrates Iceberg features, storage tiering, policy-driven retention, and query-layer masking.

## Key Concepts
- Iceberg time travel via snapshots
- Dynamic data tiering (hot/warm/cold)
- Per-tenant data masking
- Encryption at rest (KMS)
- Partition pruning and metadata caching

## Code Example
```javascript
function selectTier(tenant, ageDays) {
  const retention = (tenantRetention[tenant] || 365);
  if (ageDays < 30) return 'hot';
  if (ageDays < retention) return 'warm';
  return 'cold';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data freshness and mask correctness across tiers?
- How would you validate time travel remains accurate after tiering moves?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a scalable, production-ready ML feature store with strict data contracts, versioning, and lineage across tenants and environments.

## Key Concepts

- Data contracts and a central schema registry for versioned schemas
- Offline vs online feature stores and point-in-time correctness
- Tenant isolation and data lineage across the lakehouse
- Drift testing, schema evolution, and canary deployments
- Ingestion path choices: Spark Structured Streaming vs Flink trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: fetch feature schema and apply validation
async function validateAndRegisterFeature(event, registry) {
  const schema = await registry.getOrRegister(event.tenantId, event.featureName, event.schemaVersion);
  // Validate event fields against schema
  // Return normalized record
  return normalize(event, schema);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test tenant isolation at scale in CI/CD?
- How do you manage online/offline schema evolution and backward compatibility?
